WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.851
The sea is calm tonight.

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:06.706
A small boat, a quiet sky, and nowhere we have to be.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:09.334
From here, there are two ways to travel.

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:12.476
Up, into the air. Or down, into the water.

00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:13.869
You decide.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.232
Up, then.

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:22.569
The gulls fall away below us, and the air grows thin and cold.

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:25.939
At two thousand metres, we pass through the clouds.

00:00:26.139 --> 00:00:31.974
At ten thousand, a jet crosses our path. This is as high as most people ever get.

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:33.990
Above us, the weather ends.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.855
Two things are still higher.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.015
Twice a day, weather balloons rise from hundreds of stations around the world.

00:00:44.215 --> 00:00:48.988
This one swells as the air thins, until it is as wide as a small house.

00:00:49.188 --> 00:00:54.259
At thirty-five kilometres, the sky above is black, and the Earth below is curved.

00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:58.635
Then the balloon bursts, and its instruments drift home on a parachute.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.365
We turn north, and keep climbing.

00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:09.657
A hundred kilometres up, particles from the Sun strike the last traces of air.

00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:12.916
Oxygen glows green, and higher still, red.

00:01:13.116 --> 00:01:16.109
Nitrogen adds a fringe of blue and violet.

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:18.407
From the ground, it is a curtain.

00:01:18.607 --> 00:01:22.207
From here, it is a river of light, flowing around the pole.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.293
Down, then.

00:01:26.493 --> 00:01:30.860
The first metres are bright and busy. A shoal of fish turns as one.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.991
But water swallows light quickly. Red goes first, then yellow, then green.

00:01:36.191 --> 00:01:39.529
At two hundred metres, only a dim blue is left.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:42.091
This is where the twilight zone begins.

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:43.884
Two things go deeper.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.094
A sperm whale takes one breath, and dives.

00:01:50.294 --> 00:01:52.600
Its lungs fold flat under the pressure.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.304
For most of an hour it hunts in complete darkness, a thousand metres down and more, finding squid by sound alone.

00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:03.725
Its clicks are among the loudest sounds any animal makes.

00:02:03.925 --> 00:02:06.315
Then it turns, and rises to breathe.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.832
Below the reach of the Sun, the animals make their own light.

00:02:14.032 --> 00:02:17.738
A jellyfish flashes a ring of blue to startle a hunter.

00:02:17.938 --> 00:02:20.529
An anglerfish dangles a glowing lure.

00:02:20.729 --> 00:02:24.371
Lanternfish carry rows of tiny lights along their bellies.

00:02:24.571 --> 00:02:26.585
Most creatures down here glow.

00:02:26.785 --> 00:02:30.427
In the largest living space on Earth, light is a language.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.546
And so, back to the surface, where the boat is waiting under the stars.

00:02:38.746 --> 00:02:41.257
This was one journey. There were others.

00:02:41.457 --> 00:02:43.580
Start again, and choose differently.

